  "answer": "The implementation is complete. Here's how it satisfies each requirement:\n\n**Validation (TypeError on malformed input):**\n- Non-array input → `TypeError`\n- Each element must be an array of exactly length 2 → `TypeError`\n- Both bounds must be safe integers (`Number.isSafeInteger`) → `TypeError`\n- `start <= end` enforced → `TypeError`\n\n**No mutation:**\n- Input pairs are copied into fresh `pairs` arrays during validation, so the original array and its pairs are never touched.\n- Sorting happens on the copied `pairs`, not the input.\n- The result is built from fresh pair arrays (`[pairs[0][0], pairs[0][1]]` and `[start, end]`), so the returned result array and its pairs are all new.\n\n**Sorting:** By start, then end (`a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]`).\n\n**Merging:**\n- Overlap or adjacency handled via `start <= last[1] + 1`, so `[2,4]` and `[5,7]` merge into `[2,7]`.\n- Contained intervals don't shorten: the end is only extended when `end > last[1]`, so `[1,10]` followed by `[3,5]` stays `[1,10]`.\n\n**Empty input:** Returns `[]`.\n\n**Named export:** `export function mergeRanges` is preserved.\n\nOne note: the `+ 1` adjacency check assumes integer bounds, which is guaranteed by the safe-integer validation.",
